definitions.construction:
For \(t\in\{0,1\}\), \(x\in\mathcal B_P\), and \(h>0\), define
\[
 \widehat\mu_{t,h}(x)=
 \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf1\{(2t-1)D_{P,x,i}>0\}
 K_\triangle(|D_{P,x,i}|/h)Y_i}
 {\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf1\{(2t-1)D_{P,x,i}>0\}
 K_\triangle(|D_{P,x,i}|/h)}
\]
when the denominator is positive, and set \(\widehat\mu_{t,h}(x)=0\) otherwise.  At \(D_{P,x,i}=0\), assign the observation to neither arm.  Set
\[
 \widehat\tau^{\mathrm{LC}}_{n,h}(x)=\widehat\mu_{1,h}(x)-\widehat\mu_{0,h}(x).
\]
Strict sign, absolute value, the fixed kernel, finite sums, the positive-denominator ratio, and the zero branch make this a pointwise Borel function of \(U_{n,P}(x)\); it does not observe \(T\) separately.

statements.statement:
For every \(P\in\mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{rect}}\cup\mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{sm}}\), \(x\in\mathcal{B}_P\), \(t\in\{0,1\}\), and \(r>0\),
\[
 P\{X\in\mathcal E_{t,P}\cap B_2(x,r)\}
 =P\{T=t,\ X\in B_2(x,r)\}
 \leq\pi Lr^2.
\]

statements.proof_tex:
Fix \(P\), \(x\), \(t\), and \(r\) as in the proposed statement.  The definition of \(\mathcal E_{t,P}\), the assignment partition, and deterministic assignment give
\[
 \mathbf1\{T=t\}=\mathbf1\{X\in\mathcal E_{t,P}\}
 \quad P\text{-almost surely}.
\]
Membership in either displayed class supplies score absolute continuity and the upper density envelope.  Hence
\[
\begin{aligned}
 P\{T=t,\ X\in B_2(x,r)\}
 &=\int_{\mathcal E_{t,P}\cap B_2(x,r)}f_P(z)\,dz\\
 &\leq L\operatorname{Leb}_2\{B_2(x,r)\}
 =\pi Lr^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
The first identity also proves the displayed equality with the observed-arm event.  The almost-everywhere density bound suffices; no density floor, continuity, boundary regularity, or thickness premise is used.

statements.statement:
For every \(q\geq1\) and \(L\geq4\), there is a constant \(c_{\mathrm{CTY}}=c_{\mathrm{CTY}}(q,L)>0\) such that
\[
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}
 \left(\frac{n}{\log n}\right)^{1/4}
 \inf_{\widehat\mu_n\in\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{NP}}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{NP}}(L,q)}
 \mathbb E_P\!\left[
 \sup_{x\in\operatorname{bd}(\mathcal X_P)}
 |\widehat\mu_n(x)-\mu_P(x)|
 \right]
 =\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}}{a_n}
 \geq c_{\mathrm{CTY}}.
\]
Thus the logarithmic penalty is necessary in the coordinatewise distance decision class and on the same nonparametric law class used by Cattaneo, Titiunik, and Yu, Theorem~6; the result is not a subclass transfer from the two-sided-thickness treatment-effect model.

estimand_functional:
For each \(x\in\operatorname{bd}(\mathcal X_P)\), the observed input is \(V_{n,P}(x)=((Y_i,\lVert X_i-x\rVert_2):1\leq i\leq n)\).  The inherited coordinatewise decision class is \(\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{NP}}\), whose law-independent measurable rules satisfy \(\widehat\mu_n(x)=T_{n,x}(V_{n,P}(x))\), and the primary frontier is the explicit minimax functional
\[
 R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}
 =\inf_{\widehat\mu_n\in\mathcal T_n^{\mathrm{NP}}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{NP}}(L,q)}
 \mathbb E_P\!\left[
 \sup_{x\in\operatorname{bd}(\mathcal X_P)}
 |\widehat\mu_n(x)-\mu_P(x)|
 \right].
\]
The theorem \(\mathrm{thm:cty\text{-}same\text{-}class\text{-}log\text{-}converse}\) proves \(R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}\gtrsim(\log n/n)^{1/4}\); combined with the published uniform upper bound on this same class and decision architecture, the primary rate is \(R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}\asymp(\log n/n)^{1/4}\).  The separate observed-arm essential-limit functional for \(\tau_P\) is retained only for the secondary two-arm results.
